{"classes":["","diverted","regulated","reservoir"],"exclude":["diverted","reservoir"],"notes":{"diverted":"Hydro-diverted: most of the natural flow is taken out upstream, so the natural flood statistic overstates the flow.","regulated":"Flow controlled by a hydro scheme (storage dam, control gates or tailrace): natural flood statistics do not describe it.","reservoir":"Hydro lake or dam structure, not a river reach."},"schemes":[{"id":"kaimai","name":"Kaimai hydro scheme","operator":"Manawa Energy (formerly Trustpower)","note":"Run of river with daily storage. Western diversions: the Opuiaki Weir sends the Opuiaki River through Tunnel 4 (which also takes the Tauwharawhara Stream) into the Ngatuhoa Canal, fed too by the Ngatuhoa Weir; Tunnel 5 takes the canal to the upper Mangaonui Stream and Lake Mangaonui. Eastern diversions: the Omanawa weirs (Tunnel 2) and the Mangapapa weir on the upper Wairoa (REC2 name; Tunnel 3). The Lloyd Mandeno Canal takes Lake Mangaonui (up to 14.2 m3/s) to Lloyd Mandeno Power Station on the upper Wairoa; Lake Matariki and Lower Mangapapa lead into Lake McLaren, which also gets the Mangakarengorengo River through two tunnels (up to 7 m3/s). The Ruahihi Canal (up to 31.2 m3/s) takes Lake McLaren to Ruahihi Power Station. The Wairoa below the McLaren Falls dam had no required residual flow (150 l/s under the 2026 consents) and the Mangakarengorengo joins it just below the dam: it runs on release days (about 26 a year, the dam-release run) and when the weirs spill. EA Report 4 (2.8, pp. 10-11, Figure 2-9) confirms the stations and Lake McLaren: four stations (Lloyd Mandeno 1972, Lower Mangapapa 1979, Ruahihi 1981, Kaimai 5 1994), Lake McLaren held by a 26 m concrete arch dam across the lower Mangapapa River, a gated inlet into the 2.5 km canal to Ruahihi, and a bypass at the dam for \"recreational flows into the Wairoa River on set days each year\"; it names none of the intakes and gives no intake rate, residual flow, level or number of release days. Not modelled: the Tauwharawhara take (up to 283 l/s, a stream of about 2 km2), Lake Matariki, the short Lower Mangapapa bypass and Kaimai 5 (Report 4 does not say where it is). Intakes, flows and levels from the 2025-26 fast-track re-consenting documents (rf-6u2), read through web-search excerpts only and unconfirmed (fasttrack.govt.nz refuses scripts).","src":["https://www.manawaenergy.co.nz/kaimai-power-scheme","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaimai_hydro_power_scheme","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/HMD%20Report%204%20-%20Hydro-power%20Schemes%20Background%20and%20Descriptions.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0022/26653/Kaimai-HEPS-Appendix-A-Consent-Conditions-Final.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/12863/B.05-Appendix-F-Existing-Resource-Consents.pdf.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0022/12865/B.07-Appendix-H-Hydrology-Assessment-Tonkin-and-Taylor.pdf.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0025/18745/Kaimai-Panel-Presentation.pdf"]},{"id":"rangitaiki","name":"Aniwhenua and Matahina (Rangitaiki River)","operator":"Aniwhenua: Southern Generation; Matahina: Manawa Energy (formerly Trustpower)","note":"Aniwhenua: a barrage above the Aniwhenua Falls (Lake Aniwaniwa) diverts the river through a canal and head pond to the power station, which returns it below the falls; a compensation flow (about 2.5 m3/s) keeps the falls running. Matahina: storage dam with the station at its toe (Lake Matahina).","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aniwhenua_Power_Station","https://environment.govt.nz/assets/what-government-is-doing/Fast-Track-Unlisted/Aniwhenua-Hydroscheme/145.03-Potential-env-effects-to-be-assessed-and-management-1.pdf","https://contact.co.nz/about-us/our-story/our-powerstations/hydro/matahina"]},{"id":"wheao","name":"Wheao and Flaxy","operator":"Manawa Energy (formerly Trustpower)","note":"The Rangitaiki is dammed and all but a residual flow (0.5 m3/s) diverted by canal, with the Wheao River, to the Flaxy and Wheao stations, which discharge into the Wheao River above its confluence with the Rangitaiki.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rangitaiki_River"]}],"r":[[742,1,2,95],[743,1,2,95],[744,1,2,95],[745,1,2,95],[750,1,2,96],[751,1,2,96],[752,1,2,95],[753,1,2,95],[754,1,2,95],[755,1,2,96],[762,1,2,96],[794,1,2,100],[795,1,2,100],[796,1,2,100],[797,1,2,100],[798,1,2,100],[799,1,2,100],[800,1,2,99],[801,1,2,99],[802,1,2,99],[809,1,2,100],[820,3,2,100],[821,3,2,100],[822,3,2,100],[823,3,2,100],[885,3,2,100],[886,3,2,100],[887,3,2,100],[888,3,2,100],[1647,2,0,98],[1648,2,0,98],[1649,2,0,98],[1650,2,0,98],[1691,2,0,99],[1692,2,0,99],[1693,2,0,99],[1694,2,0,99],[1695,2,0,99],[1696,2,0,99],[1697,2,0,99],[1794,2,0,100],[1795,2,0,100],[1796,2,0,100],[1797,2,0,100],[1798,2,0,100],[1799,2,0,100],[1800,2,0,100],[1801,2,0,100],[1802,2,0,100],[1849,2,0,100],[1850,2,0,100],[1851,2,0,100],[1852,2,0,100],[1853,2,0,100],[1854,2,0,100],[1857,2,0,100],[1858,2,0,100],[1911,2,0,100],[1912,2,0,100],[1913,2,0,100],[2048,2,0,100],[2049,2,0,100],[2050,2,0,100],[2051,2,0,100],[2052,2,0,100],[2053,2,0,100],[2054,2,0,100],[2067,2,0,100],[2108,1,0,57],[2109,1,0,57],[2110,2,0,100],[2111,2,0,100],[2112,2,0,100],[2180,2,0,100],[2181,2,0,100],[2182,2,0,100],[2183,2,0,100],[2184,2,0,100],[2229,1,0,60],[2230,1,0,60],[2231,1,0,60],[2232,1,0,60],[2233,1,0,60],[2234,1,0,60],[2235,1,0,59],[2312,2,0,100],[2313,2,0,100],[2314,2,0,100],[2315,2,0,100],[2377,2,0,100],[2378,2,0,100],[2430,1,0,91],[2431,1,0,91],[2432,2,0,100],[2433,2,0,100],[2443,1,0,61],[2444,1,0,61],[2445,1,0,61],[2446,1,0,61],[2447,1,0,61],[2448,1,0,61],[2449,1,0,61],[2450,1,0,92],[2460,1,0,93],[2531,1,0,62],[2532,1,0,62],[2533,1,0,62],[2609,1,0,94],[2610,1,0,93],[2611,1,0,93],[2612,1,0,93],[2613,1,0,93],[2614,1,0,93],[2636,1,0,94],[2637,1,0,94],[2638,1,0,94],[2653,1,0,95],[2854,1,0,95],[2855,1,0